This confirms it. Commit b45c4545d introduced `loadUserTypes`, called initially with `keyspaceMetadata.getUserTypes()` which can be an empty collection. When `udts` is empty, the loop doesn't execute, `notLoaded` stays empty, and `notLoaded.size() == udts.size()` (0 == 0) is true, causing an immediate `RuntimeException("Unable to load user types []")` for keyspaces with no UDTs — exactly the bug fixed in a4ec18f80 by adding the early `if (udts.isEmpty()) return;` check.

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: b45c4545d2f4027e77dfb4fe856c42f51d897c08

## Reasoning
(1) I checked the file history of `BulkLoader.java` and found only two prior commits touching it: `354522368` and `b45c4545d`. I inspected `b45c4545d`, which is the commit that introduced the `loadUserTypes` method entirely (along with its caller `loadUserTypes(keyspaceMetadata.getUserTypes(), ksMetaData)`), as this is new code added alongside UDT support.

(2) The introduced method had this logic:
```java
if (notLoaded.size() == udts.size()) {
    throw new RuntimeException(...);
}
if (notLoaded.size() > 0) {
    loadUserTypes(notLoaded, ks);
}
```
When `udts` is empty (e.g., a keyspace has no user-defined types), the `for` loop body never executes, so `notLoaded` remains empty too. Then `notLoaded.size() == udts.size()` evaluates to `0 == 0`, which is `true`, incorrectly triggering `throw new RuntimeException("Unable to load user types []")` even though there was nothing to fail on. This is precisely the bug the fix commit `a4ec18f80` addresses by adding an early-return guard `if (udts.isEmpty()) { return; }` before the loop.

(3) The diff of `b45c4545d` shows this exact logic was introduced as brand-new code (no prior version existed), and the fix commit's diff surgically neutralizes only this empty-collection edge case without touching any other logic (it also simplifies the redundant `if (notLoaded.size() > 0)` into an unconditional recursive call, which is a safe equivalent since the empty case is now handled earlier). There is no other commit touching this method between its introduction and the fix, so I'm confident in attributing the bug to `b45c4545d`.

